      'Adhyaya 4 continues Yajnavalkya-Janaka teaching and includes another Maitreyi dialogue, analysis of waking, dream, death and the famous “neti neti” formulation. Yajnavalkya becomes the dominant voice in the most explicit self teaching.',
      'Adhyaya 5 contains shorter meditations, sacred formulas and ethical/cosmic correspondences, including the “da” story of gods, humans and demons receiving distinct imperatives—self-control, giving and compassion.',
      'Adhyaya 6 returns strongly to ritual and lineage: prana, five-fire and rebirth materials, sexual and procreative rites, teacher genealogies and transmission. The compilation therefore ends by reconnecting high self-knowledge to bodily reproduction and textual lineage.',
      'The six-adjacent architecture prevents a simplistic story in which Upanishad means ritual progressively disappears. Sacrifice, sexuality, kingship, debate and self knowledge remain intertwined to the end.'
